# 1. Platform-level incompatibilities

These are the first things to check because they determine whether your existing application can run on Spring Boot 4 at all.

## 1.1 Java version

Minimum requirement: Java 17

| Component        | Spring Boot 3.5    | Spring Boot 4.0 |
| ---------------- | ------------------ | --------------- |
| Minimum Java     | 17                 | 17              |
| Recommended Java | 21 or later        | Latest LTS      |
| Kotlin           | Depends on version | 2.2+            |
| Spring Framework | 6.2                | 7.0             |
| Spring Security  | 6.x                | 7.x             |

Java 17 applications are still supported. You do not have to upgrade to Java 21 or 25 just because you are migrating to Spring Boot 4.

However, you must review:

- Maven compiler configuration.
- Jenkins JDK installation.
- Docker base images.
- Third-party libraries' Java compatibility.
- Annotation processors and compiler plugins.

For example:

```
<properties>
    <java.version>17</java.version>
    <maven.compiler.release>17</maven.compiler.release>
</properties>
```

Migration risk: A developer may build successfully using Java 21 locally, while Jenkins still uses Java 17 or an incompatible Maven toolchain.

Make the build JDK explicit across all environments.

## 1.2 Jakarta EE 11 and Servlet 6.1

Platform breaking change

Spring Boot 4 is based on Jakarta EE 11.

Important compatibility baselines:

| Technology          | New baseline |
| ------------------- | ------------ |
| Servlet             | 6.1          |
| Tomcat              | 11.0         |
| Jetty               | 12.1         |
| JPA                 | 3.2          |
| Hibernate ORM       | 7.1 / 7.2    |
| Bean Validation     | 3.1          |
| Hibernate Validator | 9.0 / 9.1    |
| WebSocket           | 2.2          |

This matters particularly if you deploy WAR files into externally managed application servers.

For example:

- Tomcat 10.1 implements Servlet 6.0.
- Tomcat 11 implements Servlet 6.1.

You should not assume a Spring Boot 4 WAR can be deployed to your existing Servlet 6.0 container.

Another important detail: this is not another `javax.*` to `jakarta.*` migration. Spring Boot 3 already made that transition. Spring Boot 4 raises the Jakarta EE specification baseline.

You should nevertheless search for old Java EE dependencies that remain in your project.

```
grep -R "javax.servlet" src/
grep -R "javax.persistence" src/
grep -R "javax.validation" src/
```

These imports are generally expected to use the corresponding `jakarta.*` packages.

