# 2. Spring Boot 4 modularisation — one of the biggest changes

High migration impact

This is one of the most important changes that developers can underestimate.

Spring Boot 4 has restructured its internal modules into smaller, technology-focused components.

Previously, Spring Boot provided relatively large JARs containing substantial amounts of auto-configuration and related infrastructure.

Spring Boot 4 adopts a more modular architecture.

## 2.1 New module naming convention

The new convention is:

```
spring-boot-<technology>
spring-boot-starter-<technology>
```

For example:

| Technology | New module             | New starter                    |
| ---------- | ---------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| Web MVC    | spring-boot-webmvc     | spring-boot-starter-webmvc     |
| WebFlux    | spring-boot-webflux    | spring-boot-starter-webflux    |
| Security   | spring-boot-security   | spring-boot-starter-security   |
| GraphQL    | spring-boot-graphql    | spring-boot-starter-graphql    |
| JDBC       | spring-boot-jdbc       | spring-boot-starter-jdbc       |
| Redis      | spring-boot-data-redis | spring-boot-starter-data-redis |
| Flyway     | spring-boot-flyway     | spring-boot-starter-flyway     |
| Liquibase  | spring-boot-liquibase  | spring-boot-starter-liquibase  |

The module contains the relevant integration; the starter provides the convenient dependency composition.

