# 4. Jackson 2 → Jackson 3

Very high migration impact

This is probably one of the most significant application-level changes.

Spring Boot 4 uses Jackson 3 as its preferred JSON implementation.

The changes affect:

- Package names.
- Maven coordinates.
- ObjectMapper configuration.
- Custom serializers and deserializers.
- Spring MVC message converters.
- REST API request and response processing.
- Third-party libraries.

## 4.1 Package changes

Previously:

```
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.JsonNode;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.JsonSerializer;
```

Jackson 3:

```
import tools.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import tools.jackson.databind.JsonNode;
import tools.jackson.databind.JsonSerializer;
```

One important exception:

Jackson annotations remain under:

```
import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonProperty;
```

Do not perform a blind global replacement of every `com.fasterxml.jackson` import.

## 4.2 Maven dependency changes

Jackson 2:

```
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
    <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

Jackson 3:

```
<dependency>
    <groupId>tools.jackson.core</groupId>
    <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

Prefer Spring Boot's managed Jackson starter where possible.

## 4.3 Configuration API changes

Some Spring Boot Jackson integration classes have been renamed.

| Jackson 2 / Boot 3                      | Jackson 3 / Boot 4            |
| --------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| `Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilderCustomizer` | `JsonMapperBuilderCustomizer` |
| `@JsonComponent`                        | `@JacksonComponent`           |
| `@JsonMixin`                            | `@JacksonMixin`               |
| `JsonObjectSerializer`                  | `ObjectValueSerializer`       |
| `JsonValueDeserializer`                 | `ObjectValueDeserializer`     |

For example, a previous customisation:

```
@Bean
Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilderCustomizer customizer() {
    return builder ->
        builder.failOnUnknownProperties(false);
}
```

Needs to be migrated to the Jackson 3 builder customisation API.

## 4.4 Configuration properties changed

Previously:

```
spring:
  jackson:
    read:
      ...
    write:
      ...
```

Now the JSON-specific settings are under:

```
spring:
  jackson:
    json:
      read:
        ...
      write:
        ...
```

Parser-related properties also need reviewing.

## 4.5 ObjectMapper behaviour changed

Spring Boot 4 introduces format-specific mapper beans:

- `JsonMapper` for JSON.
- `XmlMapper` for XML.

Previously, defining an `ObjectMapper` bean was commonly used to customise JSON behaviour.

Now, if you want to replace an auto-configured mapper, define the appropriate `JsonMapper` or `XmlMapper` bean.

There is another subtle change: Jackson 3 automatically detects all modules on the classpath by default, whereas Boot 3 automatically registered only well-known modules.

This could change JSON serialisation behaviour without causing compilation errors.

For compatibility testing, pay particular attention to:

- Java Time serialisation.
- Enum serialisation.
- Null handling.
- Date/time formats.
- Unknown JSON properties.
- Polymorphic type handling.
- Custom serializers.
- Generic collection deserialisation.

## 4.6 Can you continue using Jackson 2?

Yes, temporarily.

Spring Boot 4 provides:

```
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-jackson2</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

However, this compatibility module is deprecated and intended as a transition mechanism.

One additional complication: Spring Boot 4's Jersey 4 integration does not yet support Jackson 3, so Jersey applications may need the Jackson 2 module.

My recommendation: Treat Jackson migration as its own workstream. Do not combine an untested Jackson 2-to-3 conversion with unrelated REST API changes.

