# 5. Spring Security 6 → 7

High security migration impact

Spring Security 7 removes several deprecated configuration APIs.

This is especially important because a migration can compile successfully but accidentally change endpoint access policies.

## 5.1 `authorizeRequests()` removed

Old configuration:

```
http
    .authorizeRequests()
        .antMatchers("/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN")
        .anyRequest().authenticated();
```

New configuration:

```
http
    .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
        .requestMatchers("/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN")
        .anyRequest().authenticated()
    );
```

Key changes:

| Old API                        | New API                            |
| ------------------------------ | ---------------------------------- |
| `authorizeRequests()`          | `authorizeHttpRequests()`          |
| `antMatchers()`                | `requestMatchers()`                |
| `and()` chaining               | Lambda DSL                         |
| `apply()` custom DSL           | `with()`                           |
| `AuthorizationManager.check()` | `AuthorizationManager.authorize()` |

## 5.2 `and()` removed from HttpSecurity DSL

Old:

```
http
    .authorizeRequests()
        .anyRequest().authenticated()
        .and()
    .formLogin()
        .and()
    .httpBasic();
```

New:

```
http
    .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
        .anyRequest().authenticated()
    )
    .formLogin(Customizer.withDefaults())
    .httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults());
```

The lambda DSL makes configuration more explicit and avoids the older chained builder pattern.

## 5.3 Custom Security DSL migration

If you implemented a custom DSL using:

```
http.apply(customDsl);
```

Migrate to:

```
http.with(customDsl, Customizer.withDefaults());
```

The old `apply()` method was deprecated before Spring Security 7.

## 5.4 AuthorizationManager changes

The old method:

```
authorizationManager.check(authentication, context);
```

has been replaced by:

```
authorizationManager.authorize(authentication, context);
```

This matters particularly if your organisation has custom authorization implementations.

## 5.5 Dispatcher type matching

Previously:

```
http.authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
    .shouldFilterAllDispatcherTypes(false)
);
```

The migration approach uses explicit dispatcher matching:

```
http.authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
    .dispatcherTypeMatchers(DispatcherType.ERROR)
    .permitAll()
);
```

This deserves careful review because error dispatches, forwards and async requests can have different security implications.

## 5.6 Spring Authorization Server

Spring Authorization Server is now part of the Spring Security portfolio.

Its dependency management has changed.

Previously, you could override:

```
<spring-authorization-server.version>
    ...
</spring-authorization-server.version>
```

The corresponding version is now managed through Spring Security's version management.

## 5.7 Security migration checklist

Review these areas:

- Multiple `SecurityFilterChain` configurations.
- Request matchers and their ordering.
- Method security (`@PreAuthorize`, `@PostAuthorize`).
- Custom `AuthorizationManager` implementations.
- OAuth2 login.
- JWT resource server.
- OAuth2 token validation.
- CSRF.
- CORS.
- Session creation policy.
- Custom authentication filters.
- Error and async dispatcher handling.
- Security test utilities.

I would prioritise security policy regression tests over merely fixing compilation errors.

