# 6. Configuration properties incompatibilities

Runtime risk

Configuration migration is particularly dangerous because incorrect properties do not always produce obvious compilation failures.

Spring Boot 4 provides a migration tool:

```
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-properties-migrator</artifactId>
    <scope>runtime</scope>
</dependency>
```

It analyses application configuration at startup and reports outdated properties. It can also temporarily migrate certain properties at runtime.

Remove it after migration.

## 6.1 Important property changes

| Area                              | Spring Boot 3.x                            | Spring Boot 4.0                                   |
| --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------- |
| Persistence exception translation | `spring.dao.exceptiontranslation.enabled`  | `spring.persistence.exceptiontranslation.enabled` |
| Spring Session Redis              | `spring.session.redis.*`                   | `spring.session.data.redis.*`                     |
| Spring Session MongoDB            | `spring.session.mongodb.*`                 | `spring.session.data.mongodb.*`                   |
| MongoDB connection settings       | `spring.data.mongodb.*`                    | `spring.mongodb.*`                                |
| MongoDB health                    | `management.health.mongo.enabled`          | `management.health.mongodb.enabled`               |
| MongoDB command metrics           | `management.metrics.mongo.command.enabled` | `management.metrics.mongodb.command.enabled`      |
| Tracing export                    | `management.tracing.enabled`               | `management.tracing.export.enabled`               |

Be careful: not every `spring.data.mongodb.*` property has moved.

For example, Spring Data-specific properties such as:

```
spring:
  data:
    mongodb:
      auto-index-creation: true
```

remain under their existing namespace.

You must distinguish MongoDB driver connection configuration from Spring Data MongoDB configuration.

## 6.2 Logback default charset

The default charset for Logback file logging is now UTF-8.

Console logging uses the console charset when available, otherwise UTF-8.

Potential impact:

- Log parsing.
- Chinese characters.
- Legacy systems expecting a specific encoding.
- Log shipping pipelines.

If your logging platform assumes another encoding, explicitly configure it.

## 6.3 DevTools LiveReload

Previously, LiveReload was enabled by default when DevTools was used.

Now it is disabled by default.

Enable it explicitly if required:

```
spring:
  devtools:
    livereload:
      enabled: true
```

## 6.4 Liveness and readiness probes

Spring Boot 4 enables liveness and readiness health groups by default.

This means:

```
/actuator/health/liveness
/actuator/health/readiness
```

are exposed as health groups by default.

For Kubernetes, this is relevant because:

- Liveness determines whether the container should be restarted.
- Readiness determines whether the Pod should receive traffic.

Review existing probe configuration, management endpoint exposure and Kubernetes manifests.

Do not automatically include every external dependency in the liveness check. A temporary database outage should not necessarily trigger a restart loop of every application instance.

