# 9. Messaging and asynchronous processing

This category is especially important for enterprise applications with Kafka, RabbitMQ and scheduled processing.

## 9.1 Spring Kafka retry changes

Spring Kafka 4 has moved retry capabilities from Spring Retry to Spring Framework.

The configuration property:

```
spring:
  kafka:
    retry:
      topic:
        backoff:
          random: true
```

has been replaced by:

```
spring:
  kafka:
    retry:
      topic:
        backoff:
          jitter: ...
```

The new jitter approach provides more flexibility.

Do not treat this as a mechanical property rename. Verify the actual retry delay distribution, backoff policy and retry behaviour.

## 9.2 Kafka Streams customization

Old:

```
StreamBuilderFactoryBeanCustomizer
```

New:

```
StreamsBuilderFactoryBeanConfigurer
```

The new configurer implements `Ordered` with a default order of zero.

If you have multiple customizers, review execution ordering.

## 9.3 Spring AMQP retry

Spring AMQP has also moved retry capabilities from Spring Retry to Spring Framework.

Two new customisation interfaces are available:

```
RabbitTemplateRetrySettingsCustomizer
RabbitListenerRetrySettingsCustomizer
```

Review:

- Retry attempts.
- Backoff intervals.
- Exception classification.
- Dead-letter routing.
- Message acknowledgement.
- Duplicate processing.
- Transaction boundaries.

## 9.4 Spring Batch 6 — a particularly dangerous change

Potential data persistence change

This is one of the changes I would place near the top of an enterprise migration risk register.

Spring Batch can now operate without a database, using in-memory metadata.

The regular starter:

```
<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-batch</artifactId>
```

uses this simplified mode.

If you upgrade without changing your dependency, your application may stop persisting Batch metadata to its existing database.

The previous arrangement can be retained by switching to:

```
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-batch-jdbc</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

Why is this dangerous?

Spring Batch uses repository metadata to track:

- Job instances.
- Job executions.
- Step executions.
- Execution status.
- Restartability.
- Execution parameters.

If you accidentally switch from JDBC metadata to in-memory metadata, restart and recovery behaviour can change significantly.

For production batch applications, this must be validated before deployment.

