# 11. Testing framework incompatibilities

High risk when automated tests are limited

Spring Boot 4 changes several testing APIs and dependency arrangements.

## 11.1 `@MockBean` and `@SpyBean` removed

Old:

```
@SpringBootTest
class UserServiceTest {

    @MockBean
    private UserRepository userRepository;
}
```

New:

```
@SpringBootTest
class UserServiceTest {

    @MockitoBean
    private UserRepository userRepository;
}
```

Similarly:

```
@SpyBean
```

becomes:

```
@MockitoSpyBean
```

There is a significant difference:

The new annotations can be used on test classes, but not in `@Configuration` classes as the old annotations could be.

If you used shared `@TestConfiguration` classes to declare mocks for multiple test suites, you need to redesign that arrangement.

## 11.2 MockitoTestExecutionListener removed

If your tests relied on Spring Boot's previous listener to initialise Mockito `@Mock` and `@Captor` fields, they may stop working.

Use Mockito's own extension:

```
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class UserServiceTest {

    @Mock
    private UserRepository repository;

    @Captor
    private ArgumentCaptor<User> captor;
}
```

## 11.3 `@SpringBootTest` no longer automatically configures MockMvc

Previously:

```
@SpringBootTest
class UserControllerTest {
    // MockMvc support
}
```

Now:

```
@SpringBootTest
@AutoConfigureMockMvc
class UserControllerTest {
}
```

## 11.4 TestRestTemplate and WebClient

`@SpringBootTest` no longer automatically provides the previous `WebClient` and `TestRestTemplate` test beans.

You need the appropriate test configuration and dependencies.

For example:

```
@SpringBootTest
@AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate
class ApiIntegrationTest {
}
```

Spring Boot also introduces `RestTestClient`, which is worth evaluating as an alternative.

## 11.5 PropertyMapping package changes

Previously:

```
org.springframework.boot.test.autoconfigure.properties.PropertyMapping
```

Now:

```
org.springframework.boot.test.context.PropertyMapping
```

The `Skip` enum also moves.

## 11.6 JUnit 6

Spring Framework 7 adopts JUnit 6 as its test baseline.

Review:

- JUnit 5-specific integrations.
- Maven Surefire and Failsafe.
- Test engines.
- Mockito.
- Spock.
- Internal testing libraries.
- Custom test extensions.

Do not assume that every third-party test plugin supports the new test stack automatically.

