# 12. Spring Boot internal APIs and custom integrations

This is particularly important if your organisation maintains internal Spring Boot libraries, common starters or auto-configuration frameworks.

## 12.1 BootstrapRegistry package changes

Previously:

```
import org.springframework.boot.BootstrapRegistry;
```

Now:

```
import org.springframework.boot.bootstrap.BootstrapRegistry;
```

Related bootstrap classes have also moved.

## 12.2 EnvironmentPostProcessor package changes

Previously:

```
import org.springframework.boot.env.EnvironmentPostProcessor;
```

Now:

```
import org.springframework.boot.EnvironmentPostProcessor;
```

If you have a custom implementation registered in:

```
META-INF/spring.factories
```

you need to review both:

- Java imports.
- Registration metadata.

The old interface remains temporarily available in deprecated form, but should not be used for new code.

## 12.3 PropertyMapper behaviour changed

This is a subtle behavioural incompatibility.

Previously, Spring Boot's `PropertyMapper` had methods for controlling whether mappings were applied when the source value was null.

The old:

```
alwaysApplyingWhenNonNull()
```

has been removed.

The new API uses:

```
always()
```

Consider:

```
mapper.from(source::getName)
      .to(destination::setName);
```

In Boot 4, if `source.getName()` returns null, the destination setter is not called.

If you need to call the setter even with null:

```
mapper.from(source::getName)
      .always()
      .to(destination::setName);
```

This matters for custom auto-configuration and property-binding infrastructure.

## 12.4 JSpecify nullability

Spring Boot 4 adopts JSpecify nullability annotations.

For example:

```
org.jspecify.annotations.Nullable
```

This can affect:

- Kotlin interoperability.
- Nullness static analysis.
- Checker Framework.
- NullAway.
- Custom annotation processors.

If your project has strict null checking, previously compiling code may now fail because API nullability contracts are more explicit.

Also review old Spring nullability annotations in:

```
org.springframework.lang
```

## 12.5 Auto-configuration classes are no longer public APIs

Spring Boot 4 removes public members, other than constants, from auto-configuration classes.

This is important for internal frameworks that directly invoke or extend Boot auto-configuration implementation classes.

For example, if your company has a shared library that imports an auto-configuration class and calls its public methods directly, you may encounter compilation failures.

Architectural recommendation: Integrate through supported extension points:

- `@ConditionalOnClass`
- `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`
- `@ConfigurationProperties`
- `@ImportAutoConfiguration`
- Customizers.
- Runtime hints.
- Application-level beans.

Avoid coupling internal libraries to implementation details of Spring Boot auto-configuration.

