# 13. Build and packaging incompatibilities

These changes are easy to overlook because they do not necessarily affect application source code.

## 13.1 Classic executable JAR loader removed

Spring Boot 4 removes support for the classic uber-JAR loader.

If your Maven build contains:

```
<loaderImplementation>CLASSIC</loaderImplementation>
```

remove it.

## 13.2 Embedded executable JAR launch scripts removed

Previously, Spring Boot supported fully executable JARs with embedded Unix launch scripts.

That functionality has been removed.

You can still build executable JARs and run:

```
java -jar application.jar
```

If you depend on embedded launch scripts, replace them with deployment scripts or another packaging mechanism.

## 13.3 Optional Maven dependencies

Another subtle change:

Optional dependencies are no longer automatically included in uber-JARs.

If your application depends on optional dependencies being packaged, review the Maven plugin configuration:

```
<includeOptional>true</includeOptional>
```

Do not assume that a dependency visible in the development classpath is necessarily included in the production artifact.

## 13.4 CycloneDX Gradle plugin

The minimum supported CycloneDX Gradle plugin version is now 3.0.0.

This matters if your CI pipeline generates SBOMs for software supply-chain compliance.

## 13.5 Maven dependency management

Spring Boot 4 has changed dependency management for several libraries.

Examples:

- Spring Retry.
- Spring Authorization Server.
- Elasticsearch low-level clients.
- Hibernate integrations.

If you use a dependency that is no longer managed by Boot, you must manage its version explicitly.

Do not blindly add the latest version. Select a version compatible with the Spring Boot 4 ecosystem.

